{
  "id": "basis-uniqueness",
  "title": "Coordinates in a Basis Are Unique",
  "eyebrow": "vector spaces",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 52,
  "topic": "Pick a basis {b₁, b₂} for the plane. Every vector v has exactly one coordinate pair (a, b) with v = a·b₁ + b·b₂. Sweeping trial (a, b) values shows only one combination lands on v. Switch to a different basis {c₁, c₂} and the same v gets new coordinates — but again, exactly one pair.",
